  "findings": {
    "sustained_rtf_below_1": {
      "metal": "NO. Median 1.3562, minimum observed 1.3055, and all 40 of 40 segments at or above 1.0. Not one segment kept up with playback.",
      "cpu": "YES. Median 0.8210, minimum 0.7992, and 33 of 35 segments below 1.0.",
      "ratio": "CPU synthesizes the same work 1.65x faster than the Metal placement on this machine."
    },
    "flat_with_length": {
      "metal": "Flat. Median RTF by audio length: 1.381 (0-2s, n=4), 1.373 (2-4s, n=6), 1.361 (4-6s, n=8), 1.334 (6s+, n=22).",
      "cpu": "Flat. 0.887 (0-2s, n=1), 0.839 (2-4s, n=6), 0.818 (4-6s, n=10), 0.839 (6s+, n=18).",
      "reading": "Neither backend degrades as the utterance grows; the difference between them is a level shift, not a slope. CPU's line sits below 1.0 across every length bucket measured, out to its longest segment at 10.697s audio (the longest Metal segment is 10.137s)."
    },
    "the_first_segment_is_the_exception": {
      "metal": "First segment of a turn: median 2.273, range 1.649-2.853. Later segments: median 1.335, max 1.720.",
      "cpu": "First segment: median 0.950, range 0.908-1.009. Later segments: median 0.810, max 1.473.",
      "reading": "Both backends pay a first-segment premium. On Metal it is enormous (1.7x the steady state) and it lands on time-to-first-audio, which is the wait a listener feels. On CPU the premium survives but the first segment still lands at or just under realtime."
    },
    "listener_consequences": {
      "median_dead_air_per_turn_s": {
        "metal": 8.9,
        "cpu": 1.8
      },
      "median_playback_start_s": {
        "metal": 22.7,
        "cpu": 12.1
      },
      "reading": "Dead air is the silence the player spends idle between sentences waiting for the next one \u2014 the direct cost of RTF above 1.0, and it falls by 80% on CPU. PlaybackStartElapsed is dominated by brain think time on a 20 tok/s local model and should not be attributed to the speech backend; it is reported for completeness only.",
      "underruns": "Zero on both legs, as expected: this worker is streaming=false, so a segment is complete before it is audible. Underruns are what a streaming path would produce, and that is precisely the path this measurement is about."
    },
    "outliers": {
      "cpu_1_47": "One CPU segment measured 1.4727 (tts-03, iteration 3, segment index 3, 6.1s of audio). It is the only CPU segment materially above 1.0; the next highest is 1.0091. Cause not established.",
      "metal_high_first_segments": "Metal's 2.66-2.85 samples are all segment index 0."
    },
    "cross_check_against_F7": "F7 measured 0.6b Metal at rtf_median 1.3289 with the out-of-tree lab harness on the same engine_sha. This run measures 1.3562 in the pipeline \u2014 2.05% apart. The instruments agree, which is the evidence that the CPU figure is a real backend difference and not a harness artifact.",
    "did_the_env_var_reach_the_worker": "Yes, by result: the same binary, fixtures and tier measured 1.36 and 0.82 with only QWEN3_TTS_BACKEND changed. The mechanism is pkg/voiceagent/tts/qwen_native_session.go building the worker environment from os.Environ() \u2014 it strips QWEN3_TTS_TIER/MODEL and rewrites the library paths, but passes everything else through. obey-voice itself never sets QWEN3_TTS_BACKEND anywhere; this run set it in the parent shell."
  },
  "what_this_does_not_settle": [
    "Audio quality. Nothing here listened to the two legs side by side. A backend that is faster and worse is not a win, and this artifact contains no evidence either way.",
    "Whether the streaming gate should change. The gate reads the installed package's backend_hint (metal here), not an ambient env var, and nothing in obey-voice sets QWEN3_TTS_BACKEND. Acting on this finding means deciding how a CPU placement gets selected and recorded, which is a separate decision this run only supplies the number for.",
    "The 1.7b tier, which is where the dead-air problem was worst. Only 0.6b was measured.",
    "Thermals and sustained load. Nine turns per leg is minutes, not an hour; a CPU backend under long use may throttle where Metal would not. Not measured.",
    "CPU cost to the rest of the machine. The CPU backend spends cores the brain also wants; on a box running a local LLM and speech at once that trade is real and is not measured here."
  ]
